<% content_for :meta_title, "Tennis Formats and Rules — GetCourt" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "Popular tennis game formats and quick rules: King of the Court, 2v2 with two balls, and 3-courts format." %>

<div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-6">
  <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border p-6 shadow-sm">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Tennis Formats & Rules</h1>

    <div class="mb-5">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1">King of the Court</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700">
        One side is the King side. Challengers rotate in and try to win points against the Kings. Winners stay on the King side; losers rotate out. Play to a target score or by timed rounds.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mb-5">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1">2 vs 2 with Two Balls</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700">
        Four players play doubles while two balls are active. Start both points at the same time and continue play safely. A team scores only when it wins both simultaneous rallies, or by agreed local rules before starting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1">3 Courts Format</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700">
        Players are split across three courts by level or current results. After each round, winners move one court up and losers move one court down. This creates balanced matches and a clear promotion/relegation flow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
